  {
    "directory": "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070457",
    "id": "q-132712",
    "status": "supported",
    "reason": null,
    "question": {
      "math_academy_id": "q-132712",
      "knowledge_point_id": "179e0482-8d67-5e28-af2d-0d3aa7f0b770",
      "knowledge_point": "Calculating a Probability Given a Normal Distribution Model",
      "problem": "In a factory, the amount of sugar on a cookie is normally distributed with a mean of $15$ grams and a standard deviation of $0.8$ grams. What is the probability that the amount of sugar on a randomly selected cookie is less than $14$ grams? Round your answer to $2$ decimal places.\n\n*Hint: A table of values of $Φ(z)$ for different values of $z$ is given below, where $Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution. *\n\n| $z$ | $\\,0.03\\,$ | $\\,0.04\\,$ | $\\,0.05\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,-1.2\\,$ | $\\,0.1093\\,$ | $\\,0.1075\\,$ | $\\,0.1056\\,$ |\n| $\\,-1.1\\,$ | $\\,0.1292\\,$ | $\\,0.1271\\,$ | $\\,0.1251\\,$ |\n| $\\,-1.0\\,$ | $\\,0.1515\\,$ | $\\,0.1492\\,$ | $\\,0.1469\\,$ |",
      "worked_solution": "Let $X$ represent the amount of sugar (in grams) on the cookies. Then we have $X∼N(15,{0.8}^{2}).$\n\nWe want to find $P(X<14).$ First, we write this as a cumulative probability:\n\n$$\nP(X<14)=P(X\\le 14)\n$$\n\nThen, we convert $X$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 14) & =P(Z\\le \\frac{14-15}{0.8}) \\\\  & =P(Z\\le -1.25) \\\\  & =Φ(-1.25)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the $z$-table, we see that\n\n$$\nΦ(-1.25)=0.1056≈0.11.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the probability that the amount of sugar on a randomly selected cookie is less than $14$ grams is approximately $0.11.$",
      "answer_fields": [
        {
          "key": "selection",
          "type": "radio",
          "choices_complete": true,
          "choices": [
