    "question": {
      "math_academy_id": "q-132860",
      "knowledge_point_id": "68479ded-5f29-5fd9-9604-e25bef7d5e32",
      "knowledge_point": "Finding a Probability When a Population Parameter is Unknown",
      "problem": "A cinema admission fee is normally distributed with a mean of $\\mu $ dollars and a standard deviation of $$0.75.$ Additionally, in $94%$ of cases the admission fee is between $$0.7\\mu $ and $$1.3\\mu .$ Find the percentage of cases when the admission fee is lower than $$5.$\n\n*Hint: A table of values of ${Φ}^{-1}(p)$ for different values of $p$ is given below, where $p=Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution. *\n\n| $p$ | $\\,0.75\\,$ | $\\,0.80\\,$ | $\\,0.85\\,$ | $\\,0.90\\,$ | $\\,0.95\\,$ | $\\,0.97\\,$ | $\\,0.99\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${Φ}^{-1}(p)$ | $\\,0.6745\\,$ | $\\,0.8416\\,$ | $\\,1.0364\\,$ | $\\,1.2816\\,$ | $\\,1.6449\\,$ | $\\,1.8808\\,$ | $\\,2.3263\\,$ |\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\n*In addition, you're given the following table of values for $p=Φ(z)$*.\n\n| $z$ | $\\,0.00\\,$ | $\\,0.01\\,$ | $\\,0.02\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,0.4\\,$ | $\\,0.6554\\,$ | $\\,0.6591\\,$ | $\\,0.6628\\,$ |\n| $\\,0.5\\,$ | $\\,0.6915\\,$ | $\\,0.6950\\,$ | $\\,0.6985\\,$ |\n| $\\,0.6\\,$ | $\\,0.7257\\,$ | $\\,0.7291\\,$ | $\\,0.7324\\,$ |",
      "worked_solution": "Let $X$ represent a randomly selected admission fee. Then we have $X∼N(\\mu ,{0.75}^{2}),$ and we want to find $P(X<5).$ But first, we need to find the mean $\\mu .$ \n\nWe know that $P(0.7\\mu <X<1.3\\mu )=0.94.$ First, we convert this to a cumulative probability:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(0.7\\mu <X<1.3\\mu ) & =0.94 \\\\ 2P(\\mu <X<1.3\\mu ) & =0.94 \\\\ P(\\mu <X<1.3\\mu ) & =0.47 \\\\ P(X<\\mu )+P(\\mu <X<1.3\\mu ) & =0.5+0.47 \\\\ P(X<1.3\\mu ) & =0.97\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we convert $X$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X<1.3\\mu ) & =0.97 \\\\ P(Z<\\frac{0.3\\mu }{0.75}) & =0.97 \\\\ Φ(\\frac{0.3\\mu }{0.75}) & =0.97 \\\\ \\frac{0.3\\mu }{0.75} & ={Φ}^{-1}(0.97) \\\\ 0.4\\mu  & ={Φ}^{-1}(0.97).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the table, \n\n$$\n{Φ}^{-1}(0.97)≈1.8808.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0.4\\mu  & =1.8808 \\\\ \\mu  & =\\frac{1.8808}{0.4} \\\\  & ≈4.702.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nFinally, we can solve for $P(X<5)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X<5) & =P(Z<\\frac{5-4.702.}{0.75}) \\\\  & ≈P(Z<0.40) \\\\  & =Φ(0.40) \\\\  & =0.6554 \\\\  & ≈65.5%\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, approximately in $65.5%$ of cases, the admission fee is lower than $$5.$",
      "answer_fields": [
